        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.741,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Quadrilaterals"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.971,
            0.551,
            0.984
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": "All these quadrilaterals are rectangles, including (iv). Quadrilateral (iv) is a rectangle because all its angles are 90°. However, it is a special kind of rectangle with all sides of equal length. We know that this quadrilateral is also called a square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.162,
            0.847,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": "**Square:** A square is a quadrilateral in which all the angles are equal to 90°, and all the sides are of equal length."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.199,
            0.848,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "Thus, every square is also a rectangle, but clearly every rectangle is not a square."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.278,
            0.253,
            0.714,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.508,
                0.26,
                0.694,
                0.328
              ],
              "content": "Wait, which one of them are you — an Indian or a Malayali? How can you be both?"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.446,
            0.848,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "This relation can be pictorially represented using a **Venn diagram**. We have seen these diagrams before. In a Venn diagram, a set of objects is represented as points inside a closed curve. Typically, these closed curves are ovals or circles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.52,
            0.681,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": "For example, the set of all squares is represented as"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.57,
            0.378,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.437,
            0.547,
            0.587,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.629,
            0.847,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": "possible squares."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.666,
            0.848,
            0.704
          ],
          "content": "Since every square is a rectangle, the Venn diagram representation of these two sets would be as follows —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.53,
            0.757,
            0.609,
            0.774
          ],
          "content": "Rectangle"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.785,
            0.463,
            0.801
          ],
          "content": "Square"
        },
